A||||21||88 Fiero||Kit Cars||||1
Z||000000||88 Fiero||10-13-1999||01:04 AM||||How do you guys feel about Kit Cars??? Reason why I am asking is because I have an opportunity to buy one and convert my car. Actually, the company where I am going to purchase this kit will even allow me to order the kit by pieces. They gave me the price break on each pieces. The Kit is a F355 and I have been eyeing on this kit for a while. It's not a stretch so there's no cutting required. It will be an inch shorter from the nose and an inch shorter from the rear. And everything comes with it. They will even cut any part that needs to be cut such as the signal lights, foglights and the grill opening. This is another reason why I haven't gone forward with my '88 Fiero co'z I really do want to do something like this. I've seen the pictures of this car and talk to guys several times... Do you guys think I should go for it??? If I do, I will take pictures of every step till it's complete. Also, if I do go forward with this...my first order will take place at the end of this month and I think I will order the part piece by piece that way I know I get everything... I will start with the bumper first and work my way all the way to the rear bumper... I think it will take me a good 6 months to complete this project...<p>[This message has been edited by 88 Fiero (edited 10-13-1999).]||||reg||1
Z||000001||DaRkLoRD||10-13-1999||01:50 AM||||I've liked kitcars for quite a while, and in the case of replicas.. the F355 is by far my favorite.. But overall, my choice is the Finale of course. I've heard kits like that can get pretty expensive tho, $45k+ with Lamborghinis, but a F355 on a stock chassis shouldn't be that much. Drop in a turbocharged 3.4L or a V8.. awesome.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Anyway, if you do decide to do this, I will definitely have room on my server for this project, if you'd like to make a website detailing the progress.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||14
Z||000002||Shiner||10-13-1999||03:07 AM||||I don't know what kind of '88 fiero you have, but DONT do it, if it's a formula or a GT, ESPECIALLY if it has t-tops!  You have every right to do it if you please, but take into consideration that you are changing one of the rarest fieros made!  If it's a coupe, go for it  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>I am personally not into kit cars.  I don't think the fiero needs to pretend to be something it's not.  A fiero is a beautiful machine, just as it is.  Besides, how would you feel if you pulled up next to a REAL 355 and they wanted to race?  What would you tell him? "My car isn't a real ferarri, it's a fake"?  And that's what I think they are.  I know I am gonna get shit for this, but you guys here are an understanding group.  If this was on the fiero.org list, I am sure I would get banned by King Peter. LOL||||reg||4
Z||000003||mwbackus||10-13-1999||04:27 AM||||Most people that own real F355's dont race their cars at stoplights.  <BR>But, if this scenario actually did occur, I would either race him just to see how fast a real 355 is, or ask him to pull over so I could get a better look at his car. hehe.  I dont think that kit cars are anything like you describe. Anyone willing to invest the time and money (which can easily exceed $30,000 and hundreds of hours) has nothing but respect in my eyes.  To me, a replica kit is an admiration of the styling of that particular car taken to its extreme, rather than just an attempt to "fake" the real thing.<P>Mike<p>[This message has been edited by mwbackus (edited 10-13-1999).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||Shiner||10-13-1999||05:41 AM||||Okay, maybe I should say a little more.  Kit cars that are COMPLEATLY original have all my respect.  It's making the car into something unlike anything else on the road.  Like the Fino, for instance.  Or a GT40 kit car.  While 355 and Countach kit cars are very nice, they just aren't my thing, like I said. <BR>And, personally, I think a fastback looks much better than a Countach anyway.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P> ||||reg||7
Z||000005||mwbackus||10-13-1999||08:45 AM||||The only "complete" kit car that I know of in current production that could qualify as  "original" is the Pisa Finale, all the rest are either psuedo-replicas or exact replicas.  I would guess that one reason for the popularity of exact replicas, is that they command a much higher resale value.  My preference would be an exact Diablo replica, but the kits are far too expensive for me to even consider at this time, and a psuedo-replica of the Diablo like the Artero, would not be an acceptable alternative. ||||reg||1
Z||000006||Cliff Pennock||10-13-1999||09:00 AM||||I like kitcars very much, too bad that most of them are based on the Fiero though... The Fiero is indeed a very unique car and not many were made in total. And every kitcar based on the Fiero means there's one less out there...||||reg||1
Z||000007||Dave85||10-13-1999||09:57 AM||||88 Fiero, where are you getting your kit from? I origanally bought my car for that purpose. I fell in love with it and scrapped the idea. Maybe with another car. Anyway, I haven't found much info on the 355 kits though. I pulled up to a real one about 6 weeks ago and thought it was awesome!<BR>Thanks, Dave||||reg||1
Z||000008||JIM GOOSE||10-13-1999||10:29 AM||||I think most every FIERO owner considers doing a kitcar off & on or maybe some other engine setup. I know that kits are a helluva lotta work, headaches & frustration. Poor quality and sloppy fit are two things that plague many of them. I always liked the F40 LOOK kits even though you pretty well knew it was a FIERO, the LOOK is the main thing. What's a little weird is that nowadays, all sorts of JAP sedans & coupes have had big wings inst on them. If you have an 88 GT,which I do, its a good looking car as it is & mimics the exotic mid-engine ITALIAN cars while keeping its integrity. If you change out the engine and the bodywork, that only leaves the spaceframe and a flat wallet. Where do you admit that you really want something different and will bust a gut to get it? I bought my car to replace a LOTUS EUROPA TC & now realize that is not possible and need to accept it as a FIERO !! By the way, you could buy an 80s ESPRIT for what you put in a kitcar and have an unusual car with lots of quirks that is FUN too.||||reg||1
Z||000009||JIM GOOSE||10-13-1999||10:29 AM||||I think most every FIERO owner considers doing a kitcar off & on or maybe some other engine setup. I know that kits are a helluva lotta work, headaches & frustration. Poor quality and sloppy fit are two things that plague many of them. I always liked the F40 LOOK kits even though you pretty well knew it was a FIERO, the LOOK is the main thing. What's a little weird is that nowadays, all sorts of JAP sedans & coupes have had big wings inst on them. If you have an 88 GT,which I do, its a good looking car as it is & mimics the exotic mid-engine ITALIAN cars while keeping its integrity. If you change out the engine and the bodywork, that only leaves the spaceframe and a flat wallet. Where do you admit that you really want something different and will bust a gut to get it? I bought my car to replace a LOTUS EUROPA TC & now realize that is not possible and need to accept it as a FIERO !! By the way, you could buy an 80s ESPRIT for what you put in a kitcar and have an unusual car with lots of quirks that is FUN too.||||reg||5
Z||000010||88 Fiero||10-13-1999||10:31 AM||||Dave...which 355 kit do you want. I know 2 different people out here in California that have a 355 kit. One is the Spyder and this guy sells it for $3,500.00. The other one makes it and sells around the same price for both Coupe and Spyder...||||reg||1
Z||000011||fieroshop||10-13-1999||10:41 AM||||Putting my 2 cents worth I have no abjection to a kit car, infact I will be building a few for resale in the very near future, since I do everything for a Fiero including paint.||||reg||1
Z||000012||88 Fiero||10-13-1999||11:22 AM||||Shiner my car is an '88 Formula in which I have already converted to a GT Fastback and have done some modifications in the body like the PISA style taillights, A molded GTR Wing with the Center adjustable blade, A Mecham Scoop, A Kid Danger Air Dam and use to have a 17 wheels all around. I pause the project because I am really eyeing the 355 kit. I know that the '88's are rare but I am not getting any younger either. Since I already took my car to next level...why not go to the next step and go all out Conversions.. at least satisfy my thirst for an exotic car....||||reg||1
Z||000013||mshill||10-13-1999||03:22 PM||||I purchased my 86 SE specifically for a kit conversion.  I have wanted to build a kit since I was 17 and couldn't talk my dad into cutting up the old 66 beetle. I have three boys 13, 11, and 9 years old and decided it would be a neat project for us all to work on.<P>I haven't decided on a kit yet, but the boys want a Countach, the wife wants a Diablo Roadster (too complex for a first kit), and I like the Roma (Ferrari Dino replica).  I will be in Pheonix next month and plan to visit Pisa to see the Artero.  It may be a less expensive, easier first kit than the Countach or Diablo.  Eventually, I will build a Diablo Roadster with a V8, but not right out of the chute.<P>I paid $750 for it and have already redone the front suspension, radiator, fuel pump, and distributor.  The body and interior are so good that many people think I should keep it and get a real junker for the project. I am seriously considering it.<P>All three of my boys have worked several hours each with me under the Fiero with me and have learned a whole lot about different parts of the cars. It's funny, I'm on my creeper and they are on skateboards.  At one point all four of us were under there pulling the fuel tank out, it got a little crowded.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000014||rcn491||10-13-1999||11:00 PM||||I am building 328 kit on a Formula. The only problem I see in your plan is ordering the kit a piece at a time. I found on my project that you often need adjacent parts to make sure the one your working on will be in alignment with the others.||||reg||1
Z||000015||lowCG||10-14-1999||12:57 AM||||Sounds like a helluva lot of work to me,I keep a mid engined Italian car under the deck,just to remind me of how much pain I'd endured for years,and just how nice it is to drive a similar car that is as reliable as an old Citation.<BR>With a flashy car come all of the problems of the "real" one,and more;risk of theft,insurance hassles,leaks,cops won't just see you as another motorist either.<BR>But that's just me,currently spending all my time getting my Fiero to work like a more expensive car.||||reg||1
Z||000016||mrfixit58||10-14-1999||08:08 AM||||mshill,<P>I like your plans... for your car AND especially, your boys. If more people got their kids involved in something productive, they would be less likely to be distructive. Good luck with both.||||reg||14
Z||000017||Dave85||10-14-1999||12:14 PM||||88, I'd have to see the spyder in person to know if I really like it. I have seen the real coupe and love it. I would like more info. on the coupe.<BR>Thanks, Dave ||||reg||1
Z||000018||88 Fiero||10-14-1999||12:34 PM||||Dave..I when I get home tonite..I will look for the guys phone no. and give it to you. Me too I'd rather have the coupe instead...||||reg||1
Z||000019||Phil||10-21-1999||09:30 AM||||If you want to build a kit car do it. I'm building a Lambo because I like the looks of it I'm not trying to fool anybody into thinking its a real Lambo. The whole idea in building any car is to please yourself not anybody else. Be prepared though it's not easy or cheap.||||reg||1
Z||000020||mshill||10-21-1999||01:45 PM||||Phil, have you selected a manufacturer for the Lambo kit?  I am looking at IFG, Euroworks and Mac's Autobody.  If I do it, it will be a non-stretch version an I wanted to do some customization like some kind of hood scoop or vents to better flow air around the radiator as well as custom lights and maybe the Pisa wraparound dash.  I don't care about being a 'replica', I just want to have fun!||||reg||1
Z||000021||88 Fiero||10-21-1999||02:06 PM||||mshill...before you decide to buy your kit... Try checking out D&R's and Gary's Custom...D&R's looks very convincing.. Reason is the bottom of the lip is almost identical as the real thing... IFG doesn't have that look...||||reg||1
